• خصص 10 دقائق يوميًا لمراجعة التكرار المتباعد.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
مراجعة الكلمات الحديثة وحدها تُعطي إحساسًا خاطئًا بالتقدم؛ الكلمات القديمة المنسية هي التي ترفع مستواك.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — راجع البطاقات المنسية أولًا.
• اليوم 3 — أعد تثبيت 5 كلمات نسيان.
• اليوم 7 — حل اختبار مفردات شامل.
• اليوم 14 — مراجعة نهائية قبل الاختبار التجريبي.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-9', 'course-grammar', 'الجمل الشرطية: الأنواع الثلاثة', 3, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الجملة الشرطية تعبّر عن علاقة شرط/نتيجة، ولكل نوع زمن خاص به: الأول للحقائق والمستقبل المحتمل، والثاني للافتراضات غير الواقعية، والثالث للتأسف على الماضي.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• النوع الأول: If + مضارع بسيط، will + فعل مجرد.
• النوع الثاني: If + ماضٍ بسيط، would + فعل مجرد.
• النوع الثالث: If + ماضٍ تام، would have + اسم المفعول.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تضع would في جملة if نفسها (لا تقل If I would study). وكلمة wish عن الماضي تتبع قاعدة النوع الثالث: I wish I had studied.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف 3 جمل شرطية حسب نوعها.
• اليوم 3 — أكمل جملة من النوع الثاني.
• اليوم 7 — حوّل wish ماضوي إلى would have.
• اليوم 14 — حل أسئلة الشرط في اختبار تجريبي كامل.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-4','q-grammar-35','q-grammar-42']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-10', 'course-grammar', 'المقارنة والتفضيل', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
للمقارنة بين شيئين نستخدم صيغة المقارنة (comparative)، وللتمييز بين أكثر من شيئين نستخدم التفضيل (superlative) مع أداة التعريف the.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المساواة: as + صفة + as (النفي: not as...as).
• المقارنة: صفة أقصر + er / more + صفة أطول.
• التفضيل: the + الأقصر + est / the most + الأطول.
• good ← better ← the best.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تقل more good بل better. ولا تخلط between as...as و so...that: as تستخدم للمساواة فقط، وso تحتاج أن تتبعها نتيجة بـ that.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — قارن شيئين بـ as...as.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال تفضيل مع the.
• اليوم 7 — صحّح more good إلى better في جملة.
• اليوم 14 — راجع صيغ المقارنة غير المنتظمة قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-6','q-grammar-15','q-grammar-23']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-11', 'course-grammar', 'حروف الجر وdespite/in spite of', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
حروف الجر تتبع الكلمة التي تسبقها، وأدوات التخالف (despite / in spite of) تأخذ اسمًا بعدها، بينما although / even though تأخذ جملة كاملة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• responsible for / good at / object to (فعل + جر محدد).
• Despite + اسم (Despite the rain...).
• In spite of + اسم (In spite of his experience...).
• Although + جملة (Although it rained...).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تتبع despite بجملة كاملة، ولا تنسَ of مع in spite of. وبعد حروف الجر دائمًا: فعل + ing (good at solving).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — راجع الأزواج: responsible for, good at.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال despite مقابل although.
• اليوم 7 — اكتب 3 جمل بـ in spite of.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة حروف الجر الشائعة في الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-16','q-grammar-24','q-grammar-26','q-grammar-36','q-grammar-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-12', 'course-grammar', 'المصدر الصريح والصفة الفعلية', 4, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
بعض الأفعال تأخذ المصدر الصريح (to do) وبعضها يلزم الصفة الفعلية (doing) وبعضها يقبل الاثنين، والفروق محفوظة وتُسأل كثيرًا في STEP.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• أفعال تأخذ -ing فقط: enjoy, suggest, avoid, mind, finish.
• بعد حرف الجر دائمًا: -ing (object to having).
• suggest + that + فعل مجرد: suggest that the report be.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تقل suggest to do بل suggest doing. ولا تستخدم to بعد حرف الجر أبدًا — حرف الجر يتبعه فعل -ing فقط.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احفظ قائمة أفعال -ing.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال enjoy + -ing.
• اليوم 7 — كوّن جملة بـ object to having.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الفرق بين to + مصدر و -ing بمثالين.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-9','q-grammar-29','q-grammar-43']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-13', 'course-grammar', 'الأزمنة المتقدمة والكلام المنقول', 7, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المستقبل التام (future perfect) يصف حدثًا يكتمل قبل نقطة مستقبلية، والكلام المنقول يزيح الأزمنة خطوة للخلف عندما يكون فعل النقل ماضيًا.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المستقبل التام: will have + اسم المفعول (will have departed).
• By the time + مضارع، جملة رئيسية مستقبل تام.
• كلام منقول ماضي: have seen ← had seen، will ← would.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
مع By the time الزمن الصحيح هو will have + ماضٍ تام وليس المضارع التام. وفي الكلام المنقول، لا تنسَ إزاحة الزمن خطوة للخلف مع فعل نقل ماضي.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — كوّن جملة بـ will have completed.
• اليوم 3 — انقل سؤالًا من المباشر إلى المنقول.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال By the time في الاختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع جدول إزاحة الأزمنة قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-27','q-grammar-31','q-grammar-33']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-14', 'course-grammar', 'الجمل الوصلية وسؤال الذيل', 6, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الجمل الوصلية (relative clauses) تربط جملة باسم سابق، وسؤال الذيل (question tag) يعكس الفعل المساعد وفاعل الجملة الأصلية.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• whose للملكية: the engineer whose design won.
• who للأشخاص، which للأشياء، where للأماكن.
• سؤال الذيل: جملة مثبتة + tag منفي (have finished → haven''t you).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تستخدم which للأشخاص ولا whose بمعنى who. وسؤال الذيل يتبع الفعل المساعد في الجملة، وليس الزمن المعنى: You''ve finished → haven''t you وليس didn''t you.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — ميّز whose عن who/which.
• اليوم 3 — كوّن سؤال ذيل لجملة مثبتة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الوصل والذيل معًا.
• اليوم 14 — راجع ضمائر الوصل الخمسة قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-5','q-grammar-19','q-grammar-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-9', 'course-reading', 'أنواع أسئلة القراءة الخمسة وترتيب الحل', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
مهما اختلفت القطعة فأسئلة القراءة خمسة أنواع فقط: المعنى، الضمير، المعلومة، الموضوع، الرأي. لا تحلها بترتيب ظهورها؛ ابدأ بالمعنى والضمير لأن مكان كل منهما محدد في النص (كلمة بخط عريض أو تحتها خط)، ثم المعلومة، ثم الموضوع، ثم الرأي.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المعنى: تراه مع mean أو closest in meaning ← اقرأ الجملة المحيطة بالكلمة.
• الضمير: يسأل what does it/they refer to ← اقرأ ما قبل الضمير فقط.
• المعلومة: لها كلمات دلالية في السؤال ← ابحث عنها في النص وأجب من جملتها.
• الموضوع: main idea / best title / purpose ← أول وآخر جملة من الفقرة، أو أول جملة من كل فقرة.
• الرأي: author''s attitude / view ← ابحث عن كلمات الحكم مثل unfortunately وshould وremarkable.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
خيار يذكر معلومة صحيحة وردت في النص لكنها جزء من الفكرة لا الفكرة العامة. في أسئلة الموضوع اختر الخيار الأشمل.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف عشرة أسئلة إلى أنواعها الخمسة دون حلها.
• اليوم 3 — حل أسئلة المعنى والضمير أولًا في قطعة كاملة.
• اليوم 7 — طبّق الترتيب الكامل في اختبار بوقت محدد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأنواع التي أخطأت فيها أكثر.

🧭 بالتكامل مع تكنيكة اسما (ASMA):
• (أ) أول سطر من كل فقرة، ثم (س) السؤال وكلماته الدلالية، ثم (م) مكان الإجابة، ثم (ا) استبعد الخيارات.
• الوقت التقريبي: دقيقة ونصف لكل سؤال شاملةً قراءة القطعة، فلا تقرأها كالجريدة.
• لا تترك سؤالًا فارغًا؛ إذا انتهى وقتك فخمّن.', array['q-reading-43','sm-reading-15','q-reading-45','q-reading-48','q-reading-22']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-10', 'course-reading', 'أسئلة المعنى والضمير: الإجابة من السياق', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
سؤال المعنى والضمير لا يحتاج قراءة القطعة كلها؛ اذهب إلى الكلمة المحددة، واقرأ جملتها، وخمّن المعنى أو المرجع بنفسك قبل أن تنظر إلى الخيارات.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المعنى: اقرأ الجملة قبل الكلمة وبعدها، وابحث عن إشارة (سبب، تضاد، مثال) تكشف معناها، ثم اختر أقرب خيار لتخمينك.
• الضمير (he, she, it, they, this, which): يعود إلى اسم مذكور قبله؛ اقرأ من بداية الجملة إلى الضمير وتجاهل ما بعده.
• طابق العدد: they تعود إلى جمع، وit إلى مفرد غير عاقل.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
خيار يشبه الكلمة في الشكل أو الصوت لا في المعنى، وخيار ضمير يعود إلى اسم أبعد من المرجع الصحيح. كلمة risk تعني مخاطرة وقد تظهر بجانب danger؛ المعنى يُحدَّد بالسياق لا بالترجمة الأولى.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — خمّن معاني خمس كلمات من سياقها قبل النظر إلى الخيارات.
• اليوم 3 — حل ثلاثة أسئلة ضمائر وتحقق من العدد.
• اليوم 7 — حلها ضمن قطعة بوقت محدد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع أي سؤال أخطأت فيه من هذا النوع.

🧭 تطبيق اسما: في هذين النوعين تُحدَّد الكلمة في السؤال، فالخطوة الثانية والثالثة شبه جاهزة؛ ركّز على الخطوة الرابعة (استبعاد الخيارات).', array['q-reading-43','q-reading-46','q-reading-49','q-reading-52','sm-reading-15','sm-reading-31']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-11', 'course-reading', 'أسئلة الموضوع والرأي: الفكرة العامة لا الجزئية', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
سؤال الموضوع يطلب الفكرة التي تغطي الفقرة أو القطعة كلها، وسؤال الرأي يطلب موقف الكاتب المفهوم من اختيار ألفاظه. الخيار الصحيح غالبًا هو الأعم، لا التفصيل الذي يذكره النص بحروفه.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• موضوع فقرة: اقرأ أول جملة وآخر جملة وخمّن الفكرة الكبرى.
• موضوع القطعة: اقرأ أول جملة في كل فقرة وربطها معًا.
• الرأي: ابحث عن كلمات الحكم والتقييم (should, unfortunately, remarkable, concern) وعن صيغ التفضيل.
• استبعد الخيار الذي يغطي فقرة واحدة فقط.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
خيار صحيح معلوماتيًا لكنه جزئي. اسأل نفسك: هل يغطي القطعة كلها أم جزءًا منها؟ وفي الرأي لا تُسقط رأيك الشخصي أو معلوماتك العامة؛ اعتمد على النص.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — لخّص فقرة في عبارة قصيرة ثم قارنها بالخيارات.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤالَي موضوع ورأي.
• اليوم 7 — طبّق الأسلوب على قطعة كاملة بوقت محدد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأسئلة التي اخترت فيها خيارًا جزئيًا.

🧭 تطبيق اسما: الخطوة الأولى (أول سطر من كل فقرة) هي الأهم هنا لأنها تبني الصورة العامة.', array['q-reading-26','q-reading-34','q-reading-45','q-reading-48','q-reading-63','q-reading-78']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-9', 'course-listening', 'روتين الـ15 ثانية قبل المقطع', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
قبل كل مقطع يُعطيك الاختبار وقتًا قصيرًا (نحو 15 ثانية لكل سؤال) لقراءة الأسئلة. هذه ثواني ذهبية: استعملها لتعرف بالضبط ما تبحث عنه، حتى تكون أذنك جاهزة لالتقاط الإجابة وقت سماعها.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اقرأ السؤال وخمّن موضوعه (من؟ متى؟ أين؟ لماذا؟).
• حدّد الخلاصة: كلمة أو كلمتان تدلانك على الإجابة.
• مرّ على الخيارات بكلمة دلالية في كل خيار فقط، ولا تقرأها كاملة.
• اترك عينيك على الأسئلة وأنت تستمع.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
قراءة الخيارات كاملة تضيّع وقتك، وتُشتّتك عن بداية المقطع. الأسئلة تأتي بترتيب الكلام نفسه.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — درّب نفسك على استخراج الخلاصة من خمسة أسئلة دون سماع.
• اليوم 3 — طبّق الروتين على مقطع واحد وأجب.
• اليوم 7 — جرّب الروتين مع قسم استماع كامل.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأسئلة التي ضاعت منك إجابتها.

🧭 تذكّر: لا تدوّن ملاحظات. ركّز على السماع وعلى الأسئلة أمامك.', array['q-listening-1','q-listening-10','q-listening-11','q-listening-13']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-10', 'course-listening', 'إعادة الصياغة واستبعاد الخيارات أثناء السماع', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المتحدث نادرًا يقول الإجابة بنفس ألفاظ الخيار؛ يُعيد صياغتها بكلمات أخرى. تعلّم أن تلتقط المعنى لا اللفظ، واستبعد الخيارات أولًا بأول أثناء الكلام.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• إذا سمعت كلمة دلالية من السؤال الثاني فقد انتهى السؤال الأول، فانتقل.
• قارن ما تسمع بالخيارات بعينيك واشطب ما قيل عكسه.
• المعلومة الصحيحة غالبًا تأتي بعد كلمات التصحيح مثل actually وbut.
• في الاستنتاج (inference) اجمع ما قيل ولا تعتمد على جملة واحدة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الذي يحتوي نفس الكلمات المسموعة قد يكون الفخ، والصحيح هو ما يطابق المعنى بكلمات مختلفة. وإذا فاتك سؤال فلا تتحسر عليه؛ تابع حتى لا تفقد الذي بعده.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اقرأ ثلاثة أسئلة، وفكّر في كيف يمكن إعادة صياغة إجاباتها.
• اليوم 3 — حل مقطعًا واستبعد الخيارات أثناء السماع.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال استنتاج بالتركيز على المعنى.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مقاطع أخطأت فيها بسبب فخ التكرار اللفظي.', array['q-listening-3','q-listening-12','q-listening-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-15', 'course-grammar', 'المعدود وغير المعدود وكلمات الكمية', 14, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الاسم المعدود يأخذ many وfew وعددًا (a student, two students)، وغير المعدود يأخذ much وlittle ولا يُجمع ولا يسبقه a. أسماء مثل information وadvice وnews وfurniture وequipment وluggage وhomework غير معدودة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• How many + معدود جمع؛ How much + غير معدود.
• a few / few + معدود؛ a little / little + غير معدود.
• some في الإثبات، any في النفي والسؤال، ولا نقول informations أو an advice.
• a lot of تصلح للاثنين.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
few وlittle بدون a تعني "قليل يكاد لا يكفي" (معنى سلبي)، أما a few وa little فتعني "قليل لكنه كافٍ". تحقق من المعنى المطلوب.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف عشر كلمات إلى معدود وغير معدود.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤالَي How much / How many.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الكمية في اختبار القواعد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الاستثناءات التي أخطأت فيها.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA): انظر إلى الخيارات أولًا؛ إذا رأيت many وmuch وfew وlittle فاعلم أن السؤال عن المعدود وغير المعدود، ثم انظر إلى الاسم بعد الفراغ.', array['sm-grammar-4','sm-grammar-5']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-water-scarcity', 'Water Scarcity in Arid Regions', 'Many countries in arid and semi-arid regions face a persistent challenge: the demand for fresh water consistently outpaces the natural supply. Traditional solutions, such as building dams and drilling deeper wells, have offered only temporary relief, as groundwater reserves take centuries to replenish naturally. In response, several governments have turned to desalination, a process that removes salt from seawater to produce potable water. While desalination plants can supply large coastal cities reliably, the process consumes enormous amounts of energy, and the leftover brine, if not managed carefully, can damage marine ecosystems. As a result, researchers are now focusing on making desalination more energy-efficient and on developing complementary strategies, such as wastewater recycling and public campaigns that encourage households to reduce daily consumption.', array['q-reading-1','q-reading-2','q-reading-3']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-remote-work', 'The Shift Toward Remote Work', 'Before 2020, remote work was often viewed as a rare privilege offered mainly to senior employees. That perception changed rapidly once large numbers of companies were forced to let employees work from home. Many organizations discovered that productivity did not decline as much as they had feared; in some cases, it even improved. However, the shift also revealed new challenges. Employees reported feeling isolated without the casual interactions of a physical office, and some managers struggled to evaluate performance without direct observation. In response, a growing number of companies have adopted hybrid models, requiring employees to come into the office only a few days a week, in an attempt to balance flexibility with the benefits of in-person collaboration.', array['q-reading-4','q-reading-5','q-reading-6']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-electric-vehicles', 'The Rise of Electric Vehicles', 'For decades, the internal combustion engine dominated the automotive industry, but the past ten years have seen a rapid shift toward electric vehicles (EVs). Government incentives, stricter emissions regulations, and falling battery costs have all contributed to this trend. Major automakers have committed billions of dollars to electrifying their vehicle lineups, and several countries have announced plans to ban the sale of new gasoline-powered cars within the next two decades. Despite this momentum, EV adoption still faces obstacles. Charging infrastructure remains limited in many regions, particularly outside major cities, and the batteries themselves require rare minerals whose extraction raises environmental and ethical concerns. Manufacturers are now investing heavily in battery recycling and in developing chemistries that rely less on scarce materials, hoping to make electric vehicles not only cleaner to drive but cleaner to produce.', array['q-reading-7','q-reading-8','q-reading-9']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-multilingual-education', 'The Value of Multilingual Education', 'Educators have long debated the best age to introduce a second language in schools. Some argue that children should focus exclusively on their native language during early education, fearing that early exposure to another language might slow down literacy development. Recent research, however, suggests the opposite: children who learn a second language early often show stronger problem-solving skills and greater cognitive flexibility than their monolingual peers. Multilingual education also appears to have long-term economic benefits, as fluency in more than one language is increasingly valued in a globalized job market. Nevertheless, implementing effective multilingual programs is not simple. It requires well-trained teachers, carefully designed curricula, and sustained investment — resources that many public school systems struggle to provide consistently.', array['q-reading-10','q-reading-11','q-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-standardized-testing', 'The Debate Over Standardized Testing', 'For over a century, standardized tests have been used to measure student achievement and to make decisions about university admissions. Supporters argue that these tests provide an objective, consistent measure that allows fair comparisons across large numbers of students, regardless of the school they attended. Critics, however, contend that standardized tests often reward test-taking strategy and short-term memorization more than genuine understanding, and that they can disadvantage students from under-resourced schools who have had less exposure to test-specific coaching. In recent years, some universities have moved to make standardized test scores optional, allowing applicants to be evaluated primarily on grades, essays, and extracurricular achievements instead. Early data on this shift has been mixed: while some institutions report more diverse applicant pools, others note that without a common benchmark, comparing students from vastly different educational backgrounds has become more difficult for admissions officers.', array['q-reading-13','q-reading-14','q-reading-15']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-vertical-farming', 'Vertical Farming in Urban Areas', 'As cities continue to expand and arable land near urban centers becomes scarcer, some entrepreneurs have turned to vertical farming — growing crops in stacked layers inside climate-controlled buildings, often located within or near the cities they supply. Because these farms use LED lighting and precisely controlled irrigation instead of relying on sunlight and rainfall, they can produce crops year-round regardless of outside weather conditions, and they typically use a fraction of the water required by traditional agriculture. Proponents also highlight the reduced transportation distance between farm and consumer, which can lower both costs and carbon emissions associated with shipping produce long distances. Nevertheless, vertical farms face a significant hurdle: the substantial electricity required to power lighting and climate control systems, which can make the produce more expensive than conventionally grown alternatives unless renewable energy sources are used. As the technology matures and energy costs fall, however, many industry analysts expect vertical farming to become an increasingly practical part of urban food supply.', array['q-reading-16','q-reading-17','q-reading-18']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-smart-cities', 'Smart Cities and Urban Data', 'Across the globe, city governments are installing networks of sensors that monitor traffic, waste collection, air quality, and energy use in real time. Proponents argue that these data allow cities to operate far more efficiently: traffic lights can adapt to congestion, garbage trucks can be dispatched only when bins are full, and buildings can automatically reduce heating when rooms are empty. Yet the shift toward data-driven urban management is not without controversy. Critics point out that collecting detailed information about how residents move through a city raises serious privacy concerns, and they warn that the same systems used to improve services could be misused to track individuals without consent. Supporters respond that the data are anonymized and protected by strict regulations, but skeptics maintain that no amount of anonymization fully removes the risk. As a result, many cities now publish the code and the data behind their smart-city programs, hoping that transparency will build the public trust that such systems ultimately depend on.', array['q-reading-19','q-reading-20','q-reading-21','q-reading-22']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sleep-academic-performance', 'Sleep and Academic Performance', 'Students often treat sleep as the first thing they are willing to sacrifice when deadlines approach, yet a growing body of research suggests that this trade-off is counterproductive. During deep sleep, the brain consolidates the information learned during the day, transferring it from short-term to long-term memory. Studies of university students have found that those who regularly sleep fewer than seven hours a night perform noticeably worse on recall tasks than their well-rested peers, even when their study time is identical. Many students try to compensate with caffeine, and while a cup of coffee can temporarily sharpen alertness, researchers note that it cannot replace the memory consolidation that only sleep provides — and consuming it late in the day can actually worsen sleep the following night. Educators increasingly encourage schools to schedule major exams later in the morning, arguing that better-rested students benefit both their grades and their health.', array['q-reading-23','q-reading-24','q-reading-25','q-reading-26']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-ocean-plastic', 'The Crisis of Ocean Plastic', 'Every year, an estimated eight million tons of plastic waste end up in the world''s oceans, where it can persist for centuries. Sunlight and wave action gradually break larger items into tiny fragments known as microplastics, which do not decompose in any practical timeframe. These fragments are frequently mistaken for food by fish, seabirds, and marine mammals, and once ingested they can travel up the food chain, eventually appearing in the seafood people consume. Scientists increasingly find microplastics in drinking water and even in human blood, raising concerns that are not yet fully understood. Governments have responded with bans on single-use plastic bags and straws, and companies have experimented with biodegradable alternatives. Yet researchers warn that recycling alone cannot solve the problem, since plastic production continues to grow faster than waste-management systems can handle it. Many argue that meaningful progress requires reducing the production of plastic in the first place, alongside better recycling infrastructure and international agreements to limit how much waste reaches the sea.', array['q-reading-27','q-reading-28','q-reading-29','q-reading-30']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-habits', 'The Power of Habits', 'Habits are behaviors that have become automatic through repetition, allowing the brain to perform them with little conscious effort. Psychologists describe a simple loop that underlies most habits: a cue, a routine, and a reward. A cue is the trigger that starts the behavior, such as the sight of a phone on the desk; the routine is the action itself, such as picking up the phone; and the reward is the pleasant feeling that follows, which encourages the brain to repeat the loop. Because this cycle is stored deep in the brain, habits are remarkably resistant to change — knowing that a habit is bad is rarely enough to break it. Research suggests that replacing an unwanted routine with a healthier one, while keeping the same cue and reward, is far more effective than trying to eliminate the cue entirely. Forming a new habit also depends heavily on repetition and on the environment: people who design their surroundings to make the desired behavior easy, such as keeping running shoes by the door, are considerably more likely to follow through than those who rely on willpower alone. Over time, small consistent changes compound into large differences in health, productivity, and learning.', array['q-reading-31','q-reading-32','q-reading-33','q-reading-34']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-public-libraries', 'Public Libraries in the Digital Age', 'A century ago, the public library was the primary place where ordinary people could access books without paying for them. Today, with information available instantly on smartphones, some observers have questioned whether libraries still matter. Library professionals respond that their institutions have evolved far beyond lending books. Modern libraries function as community hubs offering free internet access, computer training, job-search assistance, and quiet study space — services that matter most to residents who cannot afford reliable internet at home. They also host children''s programs, language classes, and meeting rooms for local groups, strengthening the social fabric of neighborhoods. Digital resources, such as e-books and online databases, have expanded the library''s reach rather than replaced it. The main threat libraries face is financial: as municipal budgets tighten, branches are sometimes closed or their hours reduced, which disproportionately affects the low-income communities that depend on them most. Supporters argue that in an age of information overload, the library''s role as a trusted, free provider of reliable information is more valuable than ever.', array['q-reading-35','q-reading-36','q-reading-37','q-reading-38']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-urban-green-spaces', 'Urban Green Spaces and Public Health', 'City planners have long viewed parks and gardens mainly as decorative additions to the urban landscape, but a growing body of research argues that they should be treated as essential infrastructure. Studies have found that neighborhoods with greater access to green spaces report lower rates of stress, anxiety, and cardiovascular disease, even after controlling for income and other socioeconomic factors. One explanation is that time spent in nature lowers levels of cortisol, the body''s primary stress hormone, and encourages physical activity such as walking and cycling. Green spaces also cool the surrounding air, which matters as heat waves become more frequent, and they absorb stormwater that would otherwise overwhelm drainage systems. Despite these benefits, low-income neighborhoods tend to have the least access to parks, often because land there is cheaper to develop for housing or industry. Some cities have responded by converting vacant lots into pocket parks and by planting trees along wide boulevards, measures that are relatively inexpensive compared with building new facilities. Researchers caution, however, that simply counting the number of parks is not enough: the quality and safety of a space determine whether residents actually use it, and a neglected park can quickly become a source of disorder rather than health.', array['q-reading-39','q-reading-40','q-reading-41','q-reading-42']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-dna-genetics', 'DNA and the Science of Heredity', 'In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick proposed the double-helix structure of DNA, the molecule that carries genetic information in living cells. DNA is organized into genes, which act as instructions for building proteins and determining traits such as eye color and height. During cell division, DNA is copied so that each new cell receives a complete set of instructions, and the discovery of how this copying works helped scientists understand how characteristics are passed from parents to offspring. Although every human cell contains roughly the same DNA, variations in specific genes explain why individuals differ, and mutations, or changes in the genetic code, can sometimes lead to diseases such as cancer. Today, researchers use this knowledge in fields ranging from forensic science, where DNA evidence helps identify suspects, to agriculture, where breeders select crops with desirable traits. The ability to read and even edit DNA has transformed medicine, but it also raises ethical questions about how far genetic intervention should go.', array['q-reading-43','q-reading-44','q-reading-45']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-space-junk', 'The Threat of Space Junk', 'Since the launch of the first satellite in 1957, thousands of objects have been placed in orbit around Earth, and many of them are no longer operational. These defunct satellites, spent rocket stages, and fragments from collisions form a growing field of space junk orbiting the planet at enormous speeds. At such velocities, even a small piece of debris can severely damage an active satellite on impact, and researchers warn that a chain reaction of collisions, known as the Kessler syndrome, could eventually make some orbits unusable. Space agencies currently track the largest pieces of debris, but smaller fragments are too numerous to monitor. Several cleanup concepts have been proposed, including nets that capture junk, robotic arms that grab defunct satellites, and materials designed to burn up completely when they re-enter the atmosphere. However, these solutions are expensive, and no international agreement yet requires countries to remove their own debris. As commercial launches multiply, scientists argue that the problem will only worsen unless future spacecraft are designed to be disposed of responsibly.', array['q-reading-46','q-reading-47','q-reading-48']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-earthquake-safety', 'Staying Safe During Earthquakes', 'Earthquakes occur when stress built up along fault lines is suddenly released, sending waves of energy through the ground. Although they cannot be predicted with certainty, scientists can estimate the probability of earthquakes in a region based on its geological history. Safety experts recommend the drop, cover, and hold on technique: people indoors should drop to their hands and knees, cover their head and neck under a sturdy table, and hold on until the shaking stops. Contrary to popular belief, standing in a doorway is no longer recommended, since doorways are often no stronger than the rest of a building. If outdoors, people should move to an open area away from buildings, trees, and power lines. After the shaking stops, the risk of aftershocks and falling debris remains, so experts advise checking for injuries, avoiding damaged structures, and listening to official announcements rather than rumors. Because earthquakes strike without warning, households in active zones are encouraged to prepare emergency kits containing water, food, medication, and important documents well in advance.', array['q-reading-49','q-reading-50','q-reading-51']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-bees-ecosystem', 'Bees and Their Role in the Ecosystem', 'Bees are among the most important pollinators on the planet, and roughly a third of the food humans consume depends on pollination by insects, birds, and bats. As bees move from flower to flower collecting nectar, pollen sticks to their bodies and is transferred between plants, enabling fruits, vegetables, and nuts to develop. In recent decades, however, bee populations in many regions have declined sharply, a phenomenon scientists refer to as colony collapse disorder. Researchers point to several contributing factors, including pesticides, loss of flowering habitats, climate change, and parasites such as the varroa mite. The decline is worrying not only for food production but also for wild plants, many of which rely on bees for reproduction. Conservation efforts now include planting native flowers, reducing the use of harmful chemicals, and supporting beekeepers who maintain healthy colonies. Scientists emphasize that protecting bees benefits agriculture directly, and they note that even small gardens can provide valuable feeding grounds for these insects throughout the growing season.', array['q-reading-52','q-reading-53','q-reading-54']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-paper-making', 'The History of Paper-Making', 'Paper is so common today that its invention is rarely considered, yet the material has a long and remarkable history. The technique of making paper is generally credited to the Chinese court official Cai Lun, who around 105 CE produced sheets from plant fibers soaked in water and pressed flat. The craft gradually spread along trade routes to the Middle East, where papermakers refined the process, and then to Europe, where early paper mills appeared in the twelfth century. For centuries, paper was made by hand from materials such as cotton and linen rags, which limited supply, but the industrial revolution brought machines that could produce long continuous sheets, lowering costs and making paper widely affordable. Modern paper is mostly made from wood pulp, a change that increased production enormously but also raised environmental concerns about deforestation. Today, recycling programs and sustainable forestry practices aim to reduce the industry''s impact, and digital technologies have reduced paper consumption for documents, even as paper remains essential for packaging, books, and hygiene products.', array['q-reading-55','q-reading-56','q-reading-57']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-king-fahd-causeway', 'The King Fahd Causeway', 'The King Fahd Causeway is a remarkable engineering project that connects the eastern coast of Saudi Arabia with the island nation of Bahrain. Construction began in 1982, and the causeway, which consists of five bridges and several artificial islands, was officially opened to traffic in 1986. Stretching about twenty-five kilometers across the Gulf, the structure was designed to withstand harsh marine conditions, including strong currents and shifting sands, and it transformed travel between the two countries by replacing a long ferry journey with a road trip of just over half an hour. The causeway has had a major economic impact, allowing goods and workers to move easily between the two countries and attracting tourism to Bahrain, which many visitors from Saudi Arabia reach by car. The artificial islands built into the structure also serve as customs and border control facilities. Over the years, the causeway has required periodic maintenance to keep its bridges and foundations in good condition, and plans have occasionally been discussed to build a parallel crossing as traffic volumes continue to grow.', array['q-reading-58','q-reading-59','q-reading-60']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-ibn-sina', 'Ibn Sina: A Pioneer of Medicine', 'Ibn Sina, known in the West as Avicenna, was a Persian physician and philosopher whose writings influenced learning for centuries. Born around 980 CE in what is now Uzbekistan, he began studying at an early age and is said to have memorized the Qur''an and mastered several fields while still young. His most famous medical work, the Canon of Medicine, organized medical knowledge of his time into a systematic form and was used as a standard textbook in European universities for over five hundred years. In it, Ibn Sina described infectious diseases, discussed the importance of observing patients, and emphasized the role of hygiene in preventing illness. Beyond medicine, he wrote on logic, mathematics, astronomy, and the nature of the soul, and his philosophy combined the ideas of earlier Greek thinkers with Islamic thought. Although some of his theories were later overturned by modern science, his insistence on careful observation and clear classification shaped the way medicine was practiced and taught for generations. His works were eventually translated into Latin and spread widely across Europe and Asia.', array['q-reading-61','q-reading-62','q-reading-63']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-ice-cream-history', 'The Long History of Ice Cream', 'Ice cream seems like a thoroughly modern treat, but its ancestors date back thousands of years. Ancient Chinese rulers enjoyed flavored ices made from snow and fruit juices, and similar chilled desserts appeared in the Middle East, where syrups flavored with fruit and rose water were cooled with ice. The treat reached Italy through trade and travel, and by the seventeenth century, recipes for frozen cream desserts were circulating among European nobility, who served them at elaborate banquets. Ice cream remained a luxury for the wealthy until the nineteenth century, when improvements in refrigeration and the invention of mechanical freezing machines allowed manufacturers to produce it in large quantities. Around the same time, vendors began selling ice cream on the streets of cities such as New York and London, making it accessible to ordinary people for the first time. Later innovations, including the waffle cone and mass-produced packaged ice cream, helped turn it into a global industry. Today, the dessert is enjoyed in nearly every country, with countless flavors, though historians note that the basic combination of milk, sugar, and ice remains remarkably unchanged from the earliest recipes.', array['q-reading-64','q-reading-65','q-reading-66']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-internet-saudi-arabia', 'The Arrival of the Internet in Saudi Arabia', 'The internet reached Saudi Arabia relatively late compared with many other countries, in large part because the government took time to plan for the service''s introduction. Academic institutions were connected in the mid-1990s through the King Abdulaziz City for Science and Technology, which established an early research network. The public internet was officially launched in 1999, and in the years that followed, the number of users grew rapidly as access prices fell and competition among providers increased. The expansion of broadband connections and, later, mobile networks brought internet access to a large share of the population, and usage became widespread among young people in particular. The growth of the internet has had a broad social impact, changing how Saudis communicate, study, shop, and access government services, many of which moved online. The authorities have also worked to expand internet coverage to remote regions, and Saudi Arabia now ranks among the countries with the highest rates of internet usage, even though penetration figures vary by age and region.', array['q-reading-67','q-reading-68','q-reading-69']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-wwii-impact', 'The Lasting Impact of World War II', 'World War II, fought between 1939 and 1945, was the deadliest conflict in human history, and its consequences shaped the postwar world in lasting ways. The war involved countries on every inhabited continent, and fighting took place on land, at sea, and in the air. Beyond the enormous human cost, the conflict left much of Europe and Asia in ruins, forcing nations to rebuild their economies and cities from the ground up. The war also produced major technological changes: radar, jet engines, and early computers were all developed or refined during the conflict and later found civilian uses. Politically, the war led to the establishment of the United Nations in 1945, an organization created with the aim of preventing future conflicts. It also redrew borders, created new states, and accelerated movements for independence in parts of the colonial world. For the countries that fought on the winning side, the conflict brought both economic growth and new global responsibilities, while defeated nations faced occupation and reconstruction. In this way, the effects of the war continue to be felt in international relations and in the daily lives of millions today.', array['q-reading-70','q-reading-71','q-reading-72']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-istanbul-history', 'The History of Istanbul', 'Few cities have occupied a more important position in world history than Istanbul, which sits on the border between Europe and Asia. The city was founded by Greek settlers around the seventh century BCE and later became Byzantium, an important trading center. Under the Roman emperor Constantine, it was renamed Constantinople and made the capital of the Eastern Roman Empire, where it remained a major center of Christianity for centuries. In 1453, the Ottoman forces under Sultan Mehmed II captured the city, and it became the capital of the Ottoman Empire, a role it held for nearly five centuries. During this period, the city flourished as a center of trade, architecture, and learning, and its skyline gained the domes and minarets for which it is famous today. After the founding of the modern Turkish Republic in 1923, the capital was moved to Ankara, but Istanbul remained the country''s largest and most economically important city. Today, it attracts millions of visitors each year, drawn by monuments that reflect its long history of conquest and renewal.', array['q-reading-73','q-reading-74','q-reading-75']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-indoor-air-pollution', 'Indoor Air Pollution', 'When people think of air pollution, they usually picture factory smokestacks and traffic, yet the air inside homes and offices is often more polluted than outdoor air. Indoor pollutants come from many sources, including cooking fumes, cleaning products, building materials, and smoke from burning fuels. Some of the most common indoor pollutants include carbon monoxide, which can be produced by faulty heaters, and volatile organic compounds, which are released by paints, carpets, and furniture. Because modern buildings are well sealed to save energy, polluted air tends to remain trapped inside, and levels of certain pollutants can become several times higher than those found outdoors. Exposure to these pollutants has been linked to headaches, respiratory problems, and worsening of conditions such as asthma, and researchers have found that the time people spend indoors means the risk is often greater than people realize. Experts recommend practical measures such as opening windows regularly, using exhaust fans in kitchens and bathrooms, choosing low-emission materials, and servicing heating equipment annually to keep pollutant levels low.', array['q-reading-76','q-reading-77','q-reading-78']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-muhammad-ali', 'Muhammad Ali: Champion Inside and Outside the Ring', 'Muhammad Ali, born Cassius Clay in 1942 in Louisville, Kentucky, is widely regarded as one of the greatest boxers of all time. He won a gold medal at the 1960 Olympic Games, and in 1964 he defeated Sonny Liston to become world heavyweight champion, an achievement he repeated several times over a career that lasted more than twenty years. Ali was known not only for his speed and technique in the ring but also for his confident personality, his poetry, and his willingness to speak about social and political issues. His refusal to serve in the American military during the Vietnam War, on religious and moral grounds, led to his boxing license being suspended and cost him several years at the peak of his career, a decision that sparked widespread debate. After he retired, Ali devoted himself to humanitarian causes and traveled widely as a goodwill ambassador, despite developing Parkinson''s disease in his later years. When he died in 2016, he was remembered around the world not simply as an athlete but as a figure who stood up for his beliefs.', array['q-reading-79','q-reading-80','q-reading-81']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-currency-history', 'The History of Coins and Currency', 'Before the invention of money, people exchanged goods directly through barter, a system that was inconvenient because it required each person to want exactly what the other offered. The first known coins appeared in the kingdom of Lydia, in what is now Turkey, around the seventh century BCE, and were made from a naturally occurring mixture of gold and silver called electrum. Because coins were stamped with the authority of the issuing ruler, their weight and value could be trusted, and the practice spread quickly across the Mediterranean and Asia. For most of history, money was based on precious metals, and the value of a coin was closely tied to the metal it contained. Paper money began as a convenient substitute, originally serving as receipts for gold deposited with bankers and later as government-issued notes. In the modern era, most money exists as electronic records in bank accounts, and central banks manage the money supply to keep prices stable. Most recently, digital currencies have appeared, but they remain a small share of the world''s money compared with traditional bank-based systems.', array['q-reading-82','q-reading-83','q-reading-84']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sugar-health-risks', 'The Health Risks of Sugar', 'Sugar is present in a surprising number of everyday foods, from soft drinks and biscuits to sauces and breakfast cereals, and health experts have become increasingly concerned about the amount people consume. The body breaks sugar down into glucose, which it uses for energy, but excess glucose that is not used is stored as fat, and over time a high-sugar diet can lead to weight gain and obesity. Sugar is also closely linked to tooth decay, since bacteria in the mouth feed on sugar and produce acids that damage enamel. More seriously, research has associated high sugar intake with type 2 diabetes and heart disease, and many scientists argue that sugary drinks are a particular concern because they are consumed quickly without making people feel full. Public health agencies now recommend that people limit added sugars to a small share of their total daily calories and suggest replacing sugary drinks with water or unsweetened alternatives. Although sugar is not harmful in very small amounts, experts emphasize that the quantities found in many processed foods make moderation difficult without conscious effort.', array['q-reading-85','q-reading-86','q-reading-87']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-1', 'DNA and the Birth of Genetics', 'The story of how scientists came to understand DNA begins in 1869, when a Swiss researcher named Friedrich Miescher, working in the German city of Tübingen, pulled an unusual substance out of the nuclei of white blood cells. He gave it the name nuclein, and later investigations showed that this material forms the core of DNA, the molecule built from a sugar-phosphate backbone and four bases that store genetic information. In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick announced the double-helix structure of DNA, explaining how genetic codes are able to copy themselves. Once this structure was understood, biologists confirmed that nuclein appears in every species, which accounts for the molecule''s stability and its power to pass traits from one generation to the next. The practical benefits soon followed: doctors used knowledge of genes to treat inherited illnesses and to design drugs suited to individual patients, while plant breeders used the same insights to develop crops that resist disease. The discovery therefore links medicine and farming through a single molecule that continues to shape both fields today.', array['sm-reading-1','sm-reading-2','sm-reading-3','sm-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-2', 'The Growing Problem of Space Junk', 'The space around Earth is becoming increasingly crowded with objects that no longer work. Old satellites, empty rocket stages, and pieces left behind by collisions all drift in orbit, and together they form what scientists call space junk. Even very small fragments are dangerous: a single bolt moving at high speed could tear through a solar panel, and engineers regularly change the course of working satellites to avoid a crash. Radar systems currently keep watch on more than forty thousand pieces of debris, though many smaller items remain untracked. To slow the growth of the problem, manufacturers now equip new spacecraft with a small extra engine. When a satellite reaches the end of its life, this engine fires downward so that the craft slows, drops, and burns up completely in the upper atmosphere instead of circling the planet for decades. The Italian Space Agency has warned that solutions must come quickly, arguing that delay could turn low-Earth orbit into a dangerous shooting gallery where one collision triggers others. International cooperation, it says, has to speed up before that nightmare becomes reality.', array['sm-reading-5','sm-reading-6','sm-reading-7','sm-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-3', 'Staying Safe in an Earthquake', 'An earthquake happens when the earth''s crust suddenly shifts, releasing a huge amount of stored energy. The shaking can make buildings collapse, roads crack, and power lines fall, and people who live in areas where earthquakes are common often feel afraid because the events are so hard to predict. Experts say that staying calm and acting quickly is essential when the ground begins to move. If you are inside a building, the best advice is to find the safest route out and leave immediately if you can. As you move, keep away from walls, windows, and heavy objects that could fall on you. Once you are outside, the safest place is an open space far from buildings and other structures that might collapse. It is natural to want to help others, but experts stress that looking after your own safety first is the key to surviving these dangerous moments.', array['sm-reading-9','sm-reading-10','sm-reading-11','sm-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-4', 'Bees and the Ecosystem', 'Bees are insects that are best known for two things: helping flowers produce fruit through pollination and making honey. Thousands of species of bees exist, and some of them live together in large social groups called colonies. These insects can be found on every continent, and their sizes vary greatly, from very tiny kinds to the much larger leaf-cutter bees. One surprising fact is that some bees, sometimes called sniffer bees, can be trained to detect particular substances, which makes them useful to researchers. Bees play a central role in the ecosystem. They make food that other creatures need, and they help trees, flowers, and other plants grow; these plants, in turn, provide food and shelter for many animals. Scientists have also studied the feelings of bees. In one experiment, bees that received an unexpected reward reacted more hopefully to new challenges, while bees that received nothing seemed less enthusiastic.', array['sm-reading-13','sm-reading-14','sm-reading-15','sm-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-5', 'The History of Paper-Making', 'Paper is so common today that it is easy to forget how long it has taken to develop. The material we use now is made by machines: trees are cut down, processed into a mixture called wood pulp, and then pressed and rolled into thin sheets. But the first man-made writing material was not paper. Around four thousand years ago, the ancient Egyptians took the fibrous stems of the papyrus plant, flattened them, laid them crosswise, and pressed them together so that they stuck. Once dried, the result was a piece of papyrus that could be written on. True paper appeared much later, in China around the year 105 CE. A man named Ts''ai Lun found a way to make it from the tough inner bark of the mulberry tree by beating the bark in water to separate the fibres. The mixture was then poured into a bamboo tray and left to dry. Chinese traders eventually carried the knowledge as far as Samarkand, where Arab merchants learned the secret and later brought it to Spain. From there, the craft spread across the rest of Europe.', array['sm-reading-17','sm-reading-18','sm-reading-19','sm-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-6', 'The Climate Zones of Saudi Arabia', 'Saudi Arabia is the largest country in the Middle East, and it covers most of the Arabian Peninsula. Because the kingdom contains three different climatic zones, the weather varies a great deal from one region to another. In the southern highlands, in a city like Abha, temperatures rarely rise above thirty-five degrees Celsius, and Abha receives more rain than any other city in the country, which is why it is known as the wettest place in the kingdom. In contrast, the eastern and central parts of Saudi Arabia suffer the highest number of dust storms each year. In places like Qassim, a dust storm can completely hide the sun, making it almost impossible to see more than a few feet ahead. The western region is different again. Makkah is considered the hottest city in Saudi Arabia, with temperatures that sometimes pass forty-eight degrees in summer. Madinah also has a hot desert climate, with very hot, dry summers and only small amounts of rain, which falls mainly between November and May.', array['sm-reading-21','sm-reading-22','sm-reading-23','sm-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-7', 'The King Fahd Causeway', 'The idea of building a bridge across the Gulf to link Bahrain and Saudi Arabia was first suggested by King Saud in 1954, though it took more than a decade to become a real project. In 1965, Sheikh Khalifa bin Salman Al Khalifa and King Faisal agreed to build the causeway, and in 1968 the two countries formed a joint committee to organize the necessary funding. The bridge was later named after King Fahd, and the name was given by Essa bin Salman Al Khalifa. During the planning stage, the World Bank provided information about the environment and geography of the region, which helped engineers design the structure. The Bahraini side of the bridge is about 4,296 metres long, and the whole crossing carries millions of passengers every year; in 2010 alone, around 19 million people travelled across it. Today the causeway stands as an important symbol of cooperation, and it is often described as a bridge between two nations.', array['sm-reading-25','sm-reading-26','sm-reading-27','sm-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-8', 'The World of Ants', 'There are about eight thousand species of ants in the world, and nearly all of them live in organized groups called colonies. Some ants are actually useful to people because they feed on the eggs and larvae of household insects such as flies, which helps keep homes clean. Ants do not need to see each other to communicate. They use chemical signals called pheromones, along with sounds and touch, and they find food mainly through their sense of smell. They also recognize other ants by smelling them. Ants eat a wide variety of things, including insects, plants, and fruits, and they come in colors such as yellow, brown, red, and black. Red ants, for example, usually live only about twenty-five to thirty days. Some ants are active mainly at night and spend the day resting in houses, palm trees, caves, or wells. Others, called meat ants, have the special job of protecting the nest. In every colony, only the queen can lay eggs, while the worker ants build the nest and collect food.', array['sm-reading-29','sm-reading-30','sm-reading-31','sm-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-9', 'How Animals Sleep', 'Animals sleep in many different ways, and each method helps them survive. Some animals sleep in groups to keep warm; lions, monkeys, and penguins all do this. The way an animal sleeps often depends on its body. Snakes, for example, cannot close their eyes or even blink, so they do not really sleep in the same way other animals do. Horses and cows also sleep with their eyes open, because they have no other choice. Other animals rest standing up. Large elephants sleep standing, while young elephants sleep in the middle of the group, lying down and protected by the bigger animals around them. Horses also sleep standing, and flamingos are famous for sleeping on just one leg. The time of day also matters. Most animals sleep at night, but nocturnal animals such as bats are active during the dark hours and sleep during the day instead.', array['sm-reading-33','sm-reading-34','sm-reading-35','sm-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-10', 'Ibn Sina: A Master of Medicine', 'Ibn Sina, known in the West as Avicenna, is one of the most famous scholars in the history of medicine. The difference between the two names simply reflects the change from one language to another. He was born in the year 980 near Bukhara in Central Asia, where his father managed a village on a royal estate. Ibn Sina began studying medicine at thirteen and became a skilled physician at sixteen, working long hours with his patients. His reputation grew so much that the Sultan of Bukhara, Nuh ibn Mansur, asked him for treatment. When the Sultan recovered, he rewarded Ibn Sina by allowing him to use the royal library, where he could study rare manuscripts. Ibn Sina wrote many books on several subjects. His most important work was an encyclopedia of medicine in five volumes called the Qanun fit Tibb, finished in 1025. He also wrote five books on law and composed poems. His books were translated into many languages, and he continued his work until his death at the age of fifty-six.', array['sm-reading-37','sm-reading-38','sm-reading-39','sm-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-1', 'How Coral Reefs Form', 'Coral reefs look like underwater gardens, but they are built by animals. Each coral polyp is a tiny, soft creature that produces a hard skeleton of limestone around its body. When polyps die, their skeletons remain, and new polyps grow on top of them. Over thousands of years, these layers pile up into enormous structures. The Great Barrier Reef in Australia, for example, stretches for more than two thousand kilometres and can be seen from space. Reefs grow best in warm, clear, shallow water, because the algae living inside the polyps need sunlight to make food. In return, the algae give the corals their bright colours. Although reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, they shelter about a quarter of all marine species. Sadly, rising sea temperatures cause corals to push out their algae, turning the reef white. This process, called bleaching, can kill an entire reef if the water stays warm for too long. Scientists therefore argue that protecting reefs must begin with limiting the heat that humans add to the planet.', array['sm2-reading-1','sm2-reading-2','sm2-reading-3','sm2-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-2', 'The Silk Road', 'The Silk Road was not a single road but a network of trade routes linking China with the Mediterranean. It began to flourish around 130 BCE, when Chinese emperors sought strong horses from Central Asia. Merchants rarely travelled the entire distance. Instead, goods passed from trader to trader, and each one added a profit before selling them onward. Silk, spices, and paper moved west, while glass, gold, and wool moved east. However, goods were not the only things that travelled. Religions, languages, and ideas moved along the same routes, and so did diseases. Buddhism, for instance, spread from India into China, carried by monks who joined the caravans. Cities such as Samarkand grew rich because they stood at the crossroads where routes met. When sea travel became faster and cheaper in the fifteenth century, the overland routes slowly declined. Yet historians agree that the exchange of knowledge along the Silk Road did more to shape the world than any single cargo of silk.', array['sm2-reading-5','sm2-reading-6','sm2-reading-7','sm2-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-3', 'The Story of the Bicycle', 'The first bicycle appeared in Germany in 1817. Karl von Drais built a wooden machine with two wheels, but it had no pedals; riders pushed along the ground with their feet. Fifty years later, French inventors attached pedals to the front wheel, and the "boneshaker" got its name from the rough ride its iron wheels produced. The next design, the high-wheeler, had an enormous front wheel that made it faster but dangerous, because riders sat high above the ground and fell forward if they hit a stone. In the 1880s the "safety bicycle" solved the problem. It had two wheels of equal size and a chain that drove the back wheel, so the rider sat low and stable. Rubber tyres filled with air arrived soon afterwards and made the ride much smoother. Because bicycles were cheap, they gave ordinary people, especially women, a new freedom to travel without depending on horses or trains.', array['sm2-reading-9','sm2-reading-10','sm2-reading-11','sm2-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-4', 'Why Young People Should Volunteer', 'Many teenagers believe that volunteering is something to do only when school work is finished. I disagree. Giving a few hours each week to a local charity or neighbourhood project is among the best investments a young person can make. First, volunteers learn practical skills such as teamwork, planning, and communication, which no textbook can fully teach. Second, helping others builds confidence, because the results are visible immediately. Third, many universities and employers notice applicants who have served their communities, so volunteering can open doors later. Critics say that students are already too busy, and they have a point: nobody should sacrifice sleep or study. Nevertheless, even one afternoon a week is enough to make a difference. Schools should therefore make time for community service instead of treating it as an optional extra.', array['sm2-reading-13','sm2-reading-14','sm2-reading-15','sm2-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-5', 'The Date Palm in Arabian Life', 'The date palm has been part of life in Arabia for thousands of years. Its fruit is rich in sugar and fibre, which gave travellers energy during long journeys across the desert. Nothing from the tree is wasted: the leaves are woven into baskets and mats, the trunk is used as timber for roofs, and even the date stones are ground and fed to animals. A mature tree can produce more than a hundred kilograms of fruit each year, although it takes several years after planting to bear its first crop. Date palms also help the environment. Their shade protects smaller crops from the harsh sun, and their roots hold the soil together in oases. Today Saudi Arabia is one of the world''s largest producers of dates, with hundreds of varieties grown across the kingdom. Each variety differs in colour, size and sweetness, and many families still have a favourite that they serve to guests.', array['sm2-reading-17','sm2-reading-18','sm2-reading-19','sm2-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-6', 'How Vaccines Work', 'A vaccine teaches the body to defend itself without making a person ill. It contains a harmless piece or a weakened form of a germ, which is enough for the immune system to recognise as an intruder. The body responds by producing antibodies, special proteins that attach to the germ and help destroy it. Crucially, the immune system also creates memory cells that remain in the blood for years. If the real germ ever enters the body, these cells recognise it quickly and trigger a fast response, often before any symptoms appear. Some vaccines need booster doses because the memory fades over time. When most people in a community are vaccinated, the disease has difficulty spreading, and this protects even those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies. Doctors call this "community immunity". Smallpox, which once killed millions, was completely wiped out in 1980 through worldwide vaccination.', array['sm2-reading-21','sm2-reading-22','sm2-reading-23','sm2-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-7', 'Why the World Has Time Zones', 'Before the late nineteenth century, every town set its clocks by the sun, so noon in one city could differ from noon in a city only fifty kilometres away. This caused little trouble until railways appeared. Timetables became confusing, and travellers sometimes missed trains simply because two stations kept different times. In 1884, delegates from twenty-five countries met in Washington and agreed to divide the world into twenty-four time zones, each roughly fifteen degrees wide, with Greenwich in London as the starting point. Most countries gradually adopted the system, although some chose unusual arrangements. China, which is geographically as wide as five zones, uses a single time across the whole country. Saudi Arabia keeps the same time everywhere, three hours ahead of Greenwich. Although the system is not perfect, it allows airlines, banks and families in different countries to plan their days with confidence.', array['sm2-reading-25','sm2-reading-26','sm2-reading-27','sm2-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-8', 'Camels: Survivors of the Desert', 'Camels have lived in the deserts of Arabia for thousands of years, and their bodies are well designed for the harsh conditions. Contrary to popular belief, a camel''s hump does not store water; it stores fat, which the animal can use as energy when food is scarce. A camel can drink up to a hundred litres of water in only a few minutes, and it can then travel for days without drinking. Its thick eyelashes and closable nostrils keep out blowing sand, and wide feet stop it from sinking into soft ground. Camels also lose very little water through sweat, because their body temperature is allowed to rise during the day before cooling at night. For centuries Bedouin families depended on camels for transport, milk and wool. Although cars have replaced them for most journeys, camels remain a proud symbol of the region, and they are still raced and bred across the kingdom.', array['sm2-reading-29','sm2-reading-30','sm2-reading-31','sm2-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-9', 'The Printing Press', 'Before the fifteenth century, books were copied by hand, a slow process that could take a monk many months to finish a single volume. As a result, books were extremely expensive, and only the church and the very rich owned them. Around 1440, a German goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press that used movable metal letters. The letters could be arranged into a page, inked, pressed onto paper, and then rearranged for the next page. Within fifty years, printing presses operated in more than two hundred European cities, and millions of books had been produced. Prices fell sharply, so ordinary families could afford them. Literacy rates rose, new ideas travelled faster, and scientists could share discoveries and correct each other''s mistakes. Some rulers feared the new technology because they could no longer control what people read. Historians often rank the printing press among the most influential inventions in history.', array['sm2-reading-33','sm2-reading-34','sm2-reading-35','sm2-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-10', 'The Rise of Solar Power', 'Solar panels convert sunlight directly into electricity using thin layers of silicon. When light strikes the silicon, it knocks electrons loose, and these electrons flow through wires as an electric current. Because the process has no moving parts and burns no fuel, a panel can work quietly for twenty-five years or more. Twenty years ago, solar power was too expensive for most people, but improved manufacturing has reduced the price of panels by roughly ninety percent. The Arabian Peninsula, with its clear skies and long days, receives some of the strongest sunlight on Earth, which makes it an ideal place for large solar farms. Saudi Arabia has announced projects capable of powering millions of homes. Nevertheless, solar power has limits. Panels produce nothing at night, so engineers are developing better batteries to store the extra energy created during the day. Until storage becomes cheaper, solar power will probably work alongside other energy sources rather than replace them.', array['sm2-reading-37','sm2-reading-38','sm2-reading-39','sm2-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-1', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Neither of the students ___ finished the assignment yet.', '[{"id":"a","text":"have"},{"id":"b","text":"has"},{"id":"c","text":"having"},{"id":"d","text":"had been"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Neither of" takes a singular verb, so "has" is correct, not "have".', 'medium', array['subject-verb-agreement']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-2', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'By the time she arrived, the meeting ___ already ___.', '[{"id":"a","text":"has / started"},{"id":"b","text":"had / started"},{"id":"c","text":"was / starting"},{"id":"d","text":"have / start"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'An action completed before another past action uses the past perfect: "had started".', 'medium', array['tenses','past-perfect']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-3', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The report was written ___ the marketing team, not the finance department.', '[{"id":"a","text":"by"},{"id":"b","text":"from"},{"id":"c","text":"with"},{"id":"d","text":"for"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passive voice agent is introduced with "by": "written by the marketing team".', 'easy', array['prepositions','passive-voice']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-4', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'If the company ___ its prices, it would attract more customers.', '[{"id":"a","text":"lowers"},{"id":"b","text":"will lower"},{"id":"c","text":"lowered"},{"id":"d","text":"had lowered"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Second conditional (hypothetical present/future): "If + past simple, would + base verb".', 'medium', array['conditionals']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-5', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'This is the engineer ___ design won the award.', '[{"id":"a","text":"who"},{"id":"b","text":"which"},{"id":"c","text":"whose"},{"id":"d","text":"whom"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"Whose" shows possession (the engineer''s design), which the other relative pronouns don''t.', 'medium', array['relative-clauses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-6', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She is ___ than her older brother at solving logic puzzles.', '[{"id":"a","text":"more good"},{"id":"b","text":"better"},{"id":"c","text":"best"},{"id":"d","text":"the better"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Good" has an irregular comparative form: "better", not "more good".', 'easy', array['comparatives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-7', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The number of applicants ___ increased significantly this year.', '[{"id":"a","text":"have"},{"id":"b","text":"has"},{"id":"c","text":"are"},{"id":"d","text":"were"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"The number of" is treated as singular ("a number of" is plural, but "the number of" is not).', 'hard', array['subject-verb-agreement']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-8', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'I would rather you ___ me before making a decision.', '[{"id":"a","text":"consult"},{"id":"b","text":"consulted"},{"id":"c","text":"will consult"},{"id":"d","text":"consulting"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Would rather + subject + past simple" expresses a present/future wish about someone else''s action.', 'hard', array['subjunctive']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-9', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She enjoys ___ new software before recommending it to clients.', '[{"id":"a","text":"to try"},{"id":"b","text":"trying"},{"id":"c","text":"try"},{"id":"d","text":"tried"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Enjoy" is always followed by a gerund ("enjoy doing"), never a to-infinitive.', 'easy', array['gerunds-infinitives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-10', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'He isn''t answering his phone; he ___ be in a meeting.', '[{"id":"a","text":"must"},{"id":"b","text":"might"},{"id":"c","text":"should"},{"id":"d","text":"needn''t"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Must" expresses a strong logical deduction based on available evidence — the most likely explanation.', 'medium', array['modals','deduction']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-11', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The new policy ___ by the board next week.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will announce"},{"id":"b","text":"will be announced"},{"id":"c","text":"is announcing"},{"id":"d","text":"announced"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The policy doesn''t announce itself — it receives the action, so the passive future ("will be announced") is correct.', 'medium', array['passive-voice','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-12', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ Nile is the longest river in Africa.', '[{"id":"a","text":"A"},{"id":"b","text":"An"},{"id":"c","text":"The"},{"id":"d","text":"(no article)"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Names of rivers take the definite article: "the Nile", "the Amazon".', 'easy', array['articles']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-13', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The workshop focused on writing clearly, editing efficiently, and ___ persuasively.', '[{"id":"a","text":"to speak"},{"id":"b","text":"speaking"},{"id":"c","text":"spoke"},{"id":"d","text":"speak"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'A list joined by "and" must use parallel grammatical forms — "writing, editing, speaking" are all gerunds.', 'medium', array['parallel-structure']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-14', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She said that she ___ the report by Friday.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will finish"},{"id":"b","text":"would finish"},{"id":"c","text":"finishes"},{"id":"d","text":"has finished"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Reported speech shifts "will" back to "would" when the reporting verb ("said") is in the past tense.', 'medium', array['reported-speech']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-15', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'This model is not ___ efficient as the previous one.', '[{"id":"a","text":"so"},{"id":"b","text":"as"},{"id":"c","text":"more"},{"id":"d","text":"very"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Equal comparisons use "as + adjective + as"; the negative form "not as...as" is standard ("not so...as" also exists but is less common).', 'medium', array['comparatives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-16', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The company is responsible ___ maintaining workplace safety.', '[{"id":"a","text":"of"},{"id":"b","text":"for"},{"id":"c","text":"with"},{"id":"d","text":"about"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Responsible" collocates with "for": "responsible for (doing) something".', 'easy', array['prepositions','collocations']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-17', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'I need to have my car ___ before the trip.', '[{"id":"a","text":"repair"},{"id":"b","text":"repaired"},{"id":"c","text":"repairing"},{"id":"d","text":"to repair"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The causative "have something done" uses a past participle: "have my car repaired".', 'medium', array['causative']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-18', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Rarely ___ such a generous offer from a competitor.', '[{"id":"a","text":"we see"},{"id":"b","text":"do we see"},{"id":"c","text":"we do see"},{"id":"d","text":"did we saw"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Negative adverbs like "rarely" at the start of a sentence require inversion: "Rarely do we see...".', 'hard', array['inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-19', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'You''ve already finished the report, ___?', '[{"id":"a","text":"haven''t you"},{"id":"b","text":"hasn''t it"},{"id":"c","text":"didn''t you"},{"id":"d","text":"aren''t you"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Question tags match the auxiliary and subject of the main clause: "You''ve finished" → "haven''t you".', 'medium', array['question-tags']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-20', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ she will accept the job offer remains uncertain.', '[{"id":"a","text":"What"},{"id":"b","text":"That"},{"id":"c","text":"Whether"},{"id":"d","text":"If"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"Whether" can introduce a noun clause in subject position; "if" normally cannot be used this way.', 'hard', array['noun-clauses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-21', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'When I was a child, I ___ live in a small village near the coast.', '[{"id":"a","text":"would"},{"id":"b","text":"used to"},{"id":"c","text":"was used to"},{"id":"d","text":"use to"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Used to" describes a past state (living somewhere); "would" is only for repeated past actions, not states.', 'medium', array['used-to-would']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-22', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The meeting had to be ___ due to unexpected bad weather.', '[{"id":"a","text":"called off"},{"id":"b","text":"called on"},{"id":"c","text":"called in"},{"id":"d","text":"called up"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Called off" is a phrasal verb meaning cancelled.', 'easy', array['phrasal-verbs']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-23', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'This is ___ most efficient method we''ve tested so far.', '[{"id":"a","text":"a"},{"id":"b","text":"an"},{"id":"c","text":"the"},{"id":"d","text":"(no article)"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Superlative forms take the definite article: "the most efficient".', 'easy', array['articles','superlatives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-24', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She''s very good ___ solving complex problems quickly.', '[{"id":"a","text":"in"},{"id":"b","text":"at"},{"id":"c","text":"on"},{"id":"d","text":"for"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Good" collocates with "at": "good at (doing) something".', 'easy', array['prepositions','collocations']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-25', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'A number of students ___ registered for the evening seminar.', '[{"id":"a","text":"has"},{"id":"b","text":"have"},{"id":"c","text":"is"},{"id":"d","text":"was"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"A number of" refers to several individual items and takes a plural verb — the opposite of "the number of", which is singular.', 'hard', array['subject-verb-agreement']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-26', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ the heavy rain, the outdoor festival continued as planned.', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although"},{"id":"b","text":"Despite"},{"id":"c","text":"In spite"},{"id":"d","text":"Even though"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Despite" is a preposition that takes a noun phrase ("the heavy rain"); "although" and "even though" require a clause, and "in spite" needs "of".', 'medium', array['prepositions','conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-27', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She asked me whether I ___ her keys anywhere.', '[{"id":"a","text":"see"},{"id":"b","text":"saw"},{"id":"c","text":"had seen"},{"id":"d","text":"have seen"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Reported speech with a past reporting verb ("asked") backshifts the tense: "Have you seen?" becomes "whether I had seen".', 'medium', array['reported-speech','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-28', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Not only ___ the cost, but the new system also shortened delivery times.', '[{"id":"a","text":"the new system reduced"},{"id":"b","text":"did the new system reduce"},{"id":"c","text":"the new system did reduce"},{"id":"d","text":"reduced the new system"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Not only" at the start of a clause triggers subject–auxiliary inversion: "Not only did the new system reduce...".', 'hard', array['inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-29', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'He suggested ___ a different strategy to solve the problem.', '[{"id":"a","text":"to try"},{"id":"b","text":"trying"},{"id":"c","text":"try"},{"id":"d","text":"tried"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Suggest" is followed by a gerund: "suggest doing", never "suggest to do".', 'easy', array['gerunds-infinitives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-30', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The more you practice, ___ you become.', '[{"id":"a","text":"the better"},{"id":"b","text":"better"},{"id":"c","text":"the best"},{"id":"d","text":"the more good"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Correlative comparatives use "the + comparative, the + comparative": "The more you practice, the better you become".', 'medium', array['comparatives','correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-31', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'By the time you receive this message, the flight ___.', '[{"id":"a","text":"departs"},{"id":"b","text":"will depart"},{"id":"c","text":"will have departed"},{"id":"d","text":"is departing"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'An action completed before a future point uses the future perfect: "will have departed".', 'medium', array['tenses','future-perfect']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-32', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Neither the report nor the presentation ___ ready in time for the review.', '[{"id":"a","text":"were"},{"id":"b","text":"have been"},{"id":"c","text":"are"},{"id":"d","text":"was"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'With "neither...nor", the verb agrees with the nearer subject — "the presentation" (singular) — so "was" is correct.', 'hard', array['subject-verb-agreement','correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-33', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'By the end of the month, the team ___ the entire training program.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will complete"},{"id":"b","text":"completes"},{"id":"c","text":"will have completed"},{"id":"d","text":"has completed"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'An action completed before a future point uses the future perfect: "will have completed".', 'medium', array['tenses','future-perfect']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-34', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The lecture was so long ___ I almost fell asleep.', '[{"id":"a","text":"that"},{"id":"b","text":"so"},{"id":"c","text":"than"},{"id":"d","text":"but"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"So + adjective + that" introduces a result clause: "so long that I almost fell asleep".', 'easy', array['result-clauses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-35', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'I wish I ___ harder for the grammar section last week.', '[{"id":"a","text":"study"},{"id":"b","text":"studied"},{"id":"c","text":"had studied"},{"id":"d","text":"have studied"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'A wish about a past action that cannot be changed uses the past perfect: "wish I had studied".', 'medium', array['wishes']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-36', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ his experience, he made several basic mistakes in the report.', '[{"id":"a","text":"Despite"},{"id":"b","text":"Although"},{"id":"c","text":"Even though"},{"id":"d","text":"Because"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Despite" takes a noun phrase ("his experience"); "although" and "even though" require a clause.', 'medium', array['prepositions','conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-37', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The manager suggested that the report ___ submitted before the board meeting.', '[{"id":"a","text":"is"},{"id":"b","text":"be"},{"id":"c","text":"was"},{"id":"d","text":"will be"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'After "suggest that", the subjunctive uses the base form of the verb: "be submitted".', 'hard', array['subjunctive']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-38', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'No sooner ___ the exam started than the power went out.', '[{"id":"a","text":"had"},{"id":"b","text":"has"},{"id":"c","text":"have"},{"id":"d","text":"was"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"No sooner + past perfect + than" describes two immediate past events: "No sooner had the exam started than...".', 'hard', array['inversion','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-39', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She is not only a talented writer ___ an excellent speaker.', '[{"id":"a","text":"but also"},{"id":"b","text":"and"},{"id":"c","text":"or"},{"id":"d","text":"with"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Not only...but also" is a correlative pair: "not only a talented writer but also an excellent speaker".', 'easy', array['correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-40', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ the fact that it was raining, the game was played as scheduled.', '[{"id":"a","text":"In spite of"},{"id":"b","text":"Even though"},{"id":"c","text":"Because"},{"id":"d","text":"So"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"In spite of" is a preposition that takes a noun phrase ("the fact that..."), unlike "even though" which needs a clause.', 'medium', array['prepositions','conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-41', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The higher the demand, ___ the prices.', '[{"id":"a","text":"the higher"},{"id":"b","text":"higher"},{"id":"c","text":"the highest"},{"id":"d","text":"the more high"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Correlative comparatives repeat "the + comparative": "The higher the demand, the higher the prices".', 'medium', array['comparatives','correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-42', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Had I known about the traffic, I ___ taken the earlier train.', '[{"id":"a","text":"would have"},{"id":"b","text":"will have"},{"id":"c","text":"would"},{"id":"d","text":"had"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Had I known" is inverted conditional; the main clause uses "would have + past participle": "I would have taken".', 'hard', array['conditionals','inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-43', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'He objected to ___ his ideas changed without discussion.', '[{"id":"a","text":"have"},{"id":"b","text":"having"},{"id":"c","text":"had"},{"id":"d","text":"to have"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Object to" is a verb + preposition combination that takes a gerund: "object to having".', 'hard', array['gerunds-infinitives','prepositions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-44', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The results of the experiment ___ published in the journal next month.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will be"},{"id":"b","text":"will"},{"id":"c","text":"are being"},{"id":"d","text":"were"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"The results" (plural subject) with the passive future: "will be published".', 'medium', array['passive-voice','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-45', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Only after she apologized ___ willing to continue the discussion.', '[{"id":"a","text":"was she"},{"id":"b","text":"she was"},{"id":"c","text":"she is"},{"id":"d","text":"is she"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Only after..." at the start triggers inversion: "Only after she apologized was she willing...".', 'hard', array['inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-1', 'reading', null, 'passage-water-scarcity', null, null, 'According to the passage, why do traditional solutions like dams offer only temporary relief?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are too expensive to build."},{"id":"b","text":"Groundwater reserves take a very long time to naturally refill."},{"id":"c","text":"Governments refuse to fund them."},{"id":"d","text":"They only work in coastal cities."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states groundwater reserves "take centuries to replenish naturally."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-2', 'reading', null, 'passage-water-scarcity', null, null, 'What is one drawback of desalination mentioned in the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It cannot supply large cities."},{"id":"b","text":"It requires drilling deeper wells."},{"id":"c","text":"It consumes a large amount of energy and produces harmful brine."},{"id":"d","text":"It only works in freshwater lakes."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage says desalination "consumes enormous amounts of energy" and the leftover brine "can damage marine ecosystems."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-3', 'reading', null, 'passage-water-scarcity', null, null, 'What are researchers currently focusing on, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Banning desalination entirely"},{"id":"b","text":"Making desalination more energy-efficient and encouraging conservation"},{"id":"c","text":"Building more dams"},{"id":"d","text":"Reducing the price of seawater"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage''s final sentence names energy-efficient desalination, wastewater recycling, and reducing consumption.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-4', 'reading', null, 'passage-remote-work', null, null, 'Before 2020, how was remote work generally perceived?', '[{"id":"a","text":"As a common practice for all employees"},{"id":"b","text":"As a rare privilege mostly for senior employees"},{"id":"c","text":"As illegal in most companies"},{"id":"d","text":"As more productive than office work"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states it was "often viewed as a rare privilege offered mainly to senior employees."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-5', 'reading', null, 'passage-remote-work', null, null, 'What challenge did the shift to remote work reveal?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Employees became less skilled."},{"id":"b","text":"Companies lost most of their clients."},{"id":"c","text":"Employees felt isolated and managers struggled to evaluate performance."},{"id":"d","text":"Office buildings had to be demolished."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage directly mentions isolation and difficulty evaluating performance as challenges.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-6', 'reading', null, 'passage-remote-work', null, null, 'What is a "hybrid model", as described in the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Working fully remotely with no office visits"},{"id":"b","text":"Coming into the office only a few days a week"},{"id":"c","text":"Working only night shifts"},{"id":"d","text":"Hiring only senior employees"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage defines it as requiring employees "to come into the office only a few days a week."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-7', 'reading', null, 'passage-electric-vehicles', null, null, 'According to the passage, what factors contributed to the shift toward electric vehicles?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Higher fuel prices alone"},{"id":"b","text":"Government incentives, stricter regulations, and falling battery costs"},{"id":"c","text":"A ban on combustion engines already in effect everywhere"},{"id":"d","text":"Improved road infrastructure"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage names "government incentives, stricter emissions regulations, and falling battery costs" as contributing factors.', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-8', 'reading', null, 'passage-electric-vehicles', null, null, 'What is one obstacle to EV adoption mentioned in the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Lack of consumer interest"},{"id":"b","text":"Limited charging infrastructure outside major cities"},{"id":"c","text":"Low resale value"},{"id":"d","text":"Excessive government taxes"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states "charging infrastructure remains limited in many regions, particularly outside major cities."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-9', 'reading', null, 'passage-electric-vehicles', null, null, 'What are manufacturers currently investing in, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cheaper gasoline production"},{"id":"b","text":"Battery recycling and chemistries that rely less on scarce materials"},{"id":"c","text":"Building more combustion engines"},{"id":"d","text":"Reducing vehicle safety standards"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage''s final sentence names "battery recycling" and "chemistries that rely less on scarce materials."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-10', 'reading', null, 'passage-multilingual-education', null, null, 'What did some educators fear about early second-language exposure?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It would improve literacy too quickly"},{"id":"b","text":"It might slow down native-language literacy development"},{"id":"c","text":"It would make children bilingual too early"},{"id":"d","text":"It would reduce cognitive flexibility"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says some feared early exposure "might slow down literacy development."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-11', 'reading', null, 'passage-multilingual-education', null, null, 'What does recent research suggest about children who learn a second language early?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They struggle with problem-solving"},{"id":"b","text":"They show stronger problem-solving skills and cognitive flexibility"},{"id":"c","text":"They lose fluency in their native language"},{"id":"d","text":"They perform worse academically overall"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states such children "often show stronger problem-solving skills and greater cognitive flexibility."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-12', 'reading', null, 'passage-multilingual-education', null, null, 'What does the passage say is required to implement effective multilingual programs?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Reducing curriculum content"},{"id":"b","text":"Well-trained teachers, careful curricula, and sustained investment"},{"id":"c","text":"Eliminating native-language instruction"},{"id":"d","text":"Shorter school days"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage lists "well-trained teachers, carefully designed curricula, and sustained investment" as requirements.', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-13', 'reading', null, 'passage-standardized-testing', null, null, 'According to supporters mentioned in the passage, what is the main advantage of standardized tests?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are cheaper to administer than other assessments"},{"id":"b","text":"They provide an objective, consistent measure for comparing students"},{"id":"c","text":"They eliminate the need for admissions officers"},{"id":"d","text":"They are only used in wealthy schools"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says supporters value tests as "an objective, consistent measure that allows fair comparisons."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-14', 'reading', null, 'passage-standardized-testing', null, null, 'What do critics of standardized testing argue, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Tests reward memorization over genuine understanding and disadvantage under-resourced students"},{"id":"b","text":"Tests are too easy for most students"},{"id":"c","text":"Tests should be mandatory for graduation"},{"id":"d","text":"Tests improve fairness in every case"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage states critics believe tests reward "test-taking strategy" and can "disadvantage students from under-resourced schools."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-15', 'reading', null, 'passage-standardized-testing', null, null, 'What has been the result of some universities making test scores optional, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"All universities have permanently abandoned testing"},{"id":"b","text":"Results are mixed — some report more diversity, others find comparisons harder"},{"id":"c","text":"Admissions became simpler for every school"},{"id":"d","text":"Test scores are now required twice"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says early data "has been mixed," citing both more diverse applicant pools and harder comparisons.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-16', 'reading', null, 'passage-vertical-farming', null, null, 'Why do vertical farms not depend on outside weather conditions, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are built underground"},{"id":"b","text":"They use LED lighting and controlled irrigation instead of sunlight and rainfall"},{"id":"c","text":"They only grow crops that don''t need water"},{"id":"d","text":"They import all their produce"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states these farms "use LED lighting and precisely controlled irrigation instead of relying on sunlight and rainfall."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-17', 'reading', null, 'passage-vertical-farming', null, null, 'What benefit related to transportation does the passage mention?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Farms are always located outside cities"},{"id":"b","text":"Reduced distance between farm and consumer can lower costs and emissions"},{"id":"c","text":"Transportation costs have disappeared entirely"},{"id":"d","text":"Produce must be shipped internationally"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage credits "reduced transportation distance" with lowering "both costs and carbon emissions."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-18', 'reading', null, 'passage-vertical-farming', null, null, 'What is described as a significant challenge for vertical farming?', '[{"id":"a","text":"A lack of available crops to grow"},{"id":"b","text":"The substantial electricity required for lighting and climate control"},{"id":"c","text":"Difficulty finding urban locations"},{"id":"d","text":"Excessive water usage compared to traditional farms"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage names "the substantial electricity required to power lighting and climate control systems" as the main hurdle.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-19', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'According to the passage, how do sensors help cities operate more efficiently?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They replace all public transportation."},{"id":"b","text":"They allow traffic lights, waste collection, and energy use to be managed based on real-time data."},{"id":"c","text":"They eliminate the need for city workers."},{"id":"d","text":"They prevent all traffic congestion."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage lists traffic lights adapting to congestion, garbage trucks dispatched when bins are full, and buildings reducing heating as examples of data-driven efficiency.', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-20', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'What concern do critics raise about smart-city systems, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The systems are too expensive to install."},{"id":"b","text":"The data collected could be misused to track individuals without consent."},{"id":"c","text":"The systems are not accurate enough to be useful."},{"id":"d","text":"The systems cause more traffic than they prevent."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Critics warn that detailed movement data "could be misused to track individuals without consent."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-21', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'How do supporters of smart-city systems respond to the privacy concerns?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They deny that any data is collected."},{"id":"b","text":"They argue the data is anonymized and protected by strict regulations."},{"id":"c","text":"They admit the concerns are valid and stop the programs."},{"id":"d","text":"They refuse to discuss the issue publicly."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Supporters respond that "the data are anonymized and protected by strict regulations," though skeptics remain unconvinced.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-22', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'What can be inferred from the fact that some cities publish the code and data behind their smart-city programs?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The cities believe public trust is essential for these systems to succeed."},{"id":"b","text":"The cities have abandoned their privacy regulations."},{"id":"c","text":"The programs are no longer collecting any data."},{"id":"d","text":"The cities hope other governments will copy the programs."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage says cities publish code and data "hoping that transparency will build the public trust that such systems ultimately depend on."', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-23', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'According to the passage, what happens during deep sleep?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The brain stops all activity."},{"id":"b","text":"The brain consolidates the information learned during the day."},{"id":"c","text":"The body produces caffeine."},{"id":"d","text":"Memory is erased to make room for new information."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states that during deep sleep "the brain consolidates the information learned during the day, transferring it from short-term to long-term memory."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-24', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'What did studies of university students find, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Students who sleep fewer than seven hours perform worse on recall tasks."},{"id":"b","text":"Students who sleep more study less effectively."},{"id":"c","text":"Sleep has no measurable effect on grades."},{"id":"d","text":"All students need exactly nine hours of sleep."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Studies found that students sleeping "fewer than seven hours a night perform noticeably worse on recall tasks than their well-rested peers."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-25', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'What limitation of caffeine does the passage mention?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It is expensive for most students."},{"id":"b","text":"It cannot replace the memory consolidation that sleep provides."},{"id":"c","text":"It causes permanent damage to the brain."},{"id":"d","text":"It is banned on most university campuses."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Researchers note caffeine "cannot replace the memory consolidation that only sleep provides."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-26', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Skipping sleep to study more is counterproductive because sleep is needed for memory."},{"id":"b","text":"Caffeine is the most reliable way to improve exam performance."},{"id":"c","text":"Universities should assign less homework to all students."},{"id":"d","text":"Studying at night is more effective than studying in the morning."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage argues that sacrificing sleep is "counterproductive" because sleep is required for memory consolidation, which caffeine cannot replace.', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','main-idea']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-27', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'According to the passage, why can plastic waste persist in the ocean for centuries?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It sinks too quickly for organisms to remove it."},{"id":"b","text":"It does not decompose within a practical timeframe."},{"id":"c","text":"Governments forbid anyone from cleaning it up."},{"id":"d","text":"It only forms in very deep water."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says plastic breaks into microplastics that "do not decompose in any practical timeframe."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-28', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'How do microplastics reach humans, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Through food chains, once marine animals ingest the fragments."},{"id":"b","text":"Only by drinking water from rivers."},{"id":"c","text":"They cannot reach humans at all."},{"id":"d","text":"Through direct contact with plastic bags."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage states ingested microplastics "can travel up the food chain, eventually appearing in the seafood people consume."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-29', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'What do researchers argue about recycling, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Recycling alone cannot solve the problem because plastic production keeps growing."},{"id":"b","text":"Recycling is the only effective solution."},{"id":"c","text":"Recycling is too expensive to attempt."},{"id":"d","text":"Recycling has made the problem worse."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage says recycling alone cannot solve the problem "since plastic production continues to grow faster than waste-management systems can handle it."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-30', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'What does the passage suggest is needed for meaningful progress against ocean plastic?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Burning plastic waste at sea"},{"id":"b","text":"Reducing plastic production and improving recycling infrastructure"},{"id":"c","text":"Increasing the use of single-use plastic bags"},{"id":"d","text":"Banning all fishing worldwide"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage concludes that progress "requires reducing the production of plastic in the first place, alongside better recycling infrastructure and international agreements."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-31', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'According to the passage, which part of the habit loop is the trigger that starts the behavior?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The reward"},{"id":"b","text":"The routine"},{"id":"c","text":"The cue"},{"id":"d","text":"The willpower"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage defines a cue as "the trigger that starts the behavior."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-32', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'What does research suggest is more effective than trying to eliminate the cue of a bad habit?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ignoring the reward entirely"},{"id":"b","text":"Replacing the routine while keeping the same cue and reward"},{"id":"c","text":"Punishing yourself after each relapse"},{"id":"d","text":"Studying the habit in detail for months"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says "replacing an unwanted routine with a healthier one, while keeping the same cue and reward, is far more effective."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-33', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'Why do people who design their environment find it easier to form new habits, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Their environment makes the desired behavior easy to do."},{"id":"b","text":"They have more willpower than other people."},{"id":"c","text":"Their habits are stored deeper in the brain."},{"id":"d","text":"They avoid all cues completely."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage says people who "design their surroundings to make the desired behavior easy" are more likely to follow through than those relying on willpower alone.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-34', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Habits can never be changed once they are formed."},{"id":"b","text":"Habits are automatic behaviors that can be reshaped through cue, routine, and reward."},{"id":"c","text":"Willpower is the single most important factor in learning."},{"id":"d","text":"Rewards are unnecessary for forming new habits."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage explains the cue-routine-reward loop and how understanding it helps people reshape their habits over time.', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','main-idea']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-35', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'How have modern libraries changed beyond lending books, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They now charge fees for every service."},{"id":"b","text":"They act as community hubs offering internet, training, and study space."},{"id":"c","text":"They stopped offering free access entirely."},{"id":"d","text":"They only serve wealthy neighborhoods."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage describes modern libraries as community hubs offering free internet, computer training, job-search assistance, and quiet study space.', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-36', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'Which groups depend on libraries the most, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Residents who cannot afford reliable home internet"},{"id":"b","text":"Tourists visiting the city"},{"id":"c","text":"Employees of tech companies"},{"id":"d","text":"People who read only on smartphones"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage says libraries matter most to residents "who cannot afford reliable internet at home."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-37', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'What does the passage identify as the main threat facing libraries?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Too many books going out of print"},{"id":"b","text":"Competition from smartphone screens"},{"id":"c","text":"Financial pressure as municipal budgets tighten"},{"id":"d","text":"A lack of trained librarians"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage states the main threat is financial: "as municipal budgets tighten, branches are sometimes closed or their hours reduced."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-38', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'Which statement best reflects the author''s view of public libraries?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Libraries are outdated and should close."},{"id":"b","text":"Libraries remain valuable as free, trusted providers of information and services."},{"id":"c","text":"Libraries should focus only on lending printed books."},{"id":"d","text":"Libraries will be replaced by private companies."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Supporters (with whom the passage aligns) argue "the library''s role as a trusted, free provider of reliable information is more valuable than ever."', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-39', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'According to the passage, how have city planners traditionally viewed parks?', '[{"id":"a","text":"As essential infrastructure on par with roads"},{"id":"b","text":"As mainly decorative additions to the urban landscape"},{"id":"c","text":"As sources of tax revenue"},{"id":"d","text":"As a solution to housing shortages"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage opens by saying planners "have long viewed parks and gardens mainly as decorative additions."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-40', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'What is one explanation the passage gives for why green spaces improve health?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They attract new residents to the neighborhood."},{"id":"b","text":"Time in nature lowers stress hormones and encourages physical activity."},{"id":"c","text":"They raise property prices and local tax income."},{"id":"d","text":"They replace the need for hospitals."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage cites lower cortisol levels and increased walking and cycling as the health link.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-41', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'Why does the passage say low-income neighborhoods often have the least access to parks?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Residents there prefer private gardens."},{"id":"b","text":"Government regulations ban new parks in those areas."},{"id":"c","text":"Land there is cheaper to develop for housing or industry."},{"id":"d","text":"Residents have no interest in outdoor activities."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage says land in these neighborhoods is often "cheaper to develop for housing or industry."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-42', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'Which caution does the author raise at the end of the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Counting parks alone is not enough because quality and safety determine actual use."},{"id":"b","text":"Cities should stop planting trees along boulevards."},{"id":"c","text":"Green spaces cause more cardiovascular disease."},{"id":"d","text":"Pocket parks are too expensive to build."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The author warns that "the quality and safety of a space determine whether residents actually use it."', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-listening-1', 'listening', null, null, '/audio/listening/q-listening-1.mp3', 'Man: Excuse me, does this bus go to the central library?
Woman: No, you need the number 12. This one goes to the airport.
Man: Oh, I see. Where can I catch the number 12?
Woman: Just across the street, at the stop next to the pharmacy.', 'Where does the woman tell the man to catch the number 12 bus?', '[{"id":"a","text":"At the airport"},{"id":"b","text":"At the central library"},{"id":"c","text":"Across the street, next to the pharmacy"},{"id":"d","text":"At the same stop"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The woman says the stop is "across the street, at the stop next to the pharmacy."', 'easy', array['listening-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
